/obj/item/book/fission_guide
	name = "Guide to Fission Power"
	title = "RBMK-Type Fission Reactor: Operator's Manual"
	author = "Nanotrasen Nuclear Engineering Division"
	icon = 'icons/obj/book.dmi'
	icon_state = "book_eng"
	raw_text = {"
<center><b>Operator's Manual: RBMK-Type Fission Reactor</b></center><br><br>
<b><u>1. Introduction</u></b><br>
The RBMK-type reactor is a high-power, graphite-moderated nuclear fission reactor. When operated correctly, it is capable of supplying immense power to the station. When operated incorrectly, it is capable of immense destruction.<br><br>
<b><u>2. Construction</u></b><br>
The reactor is a multi-tile structure built from several key components:
<ul><li><b>Reactor Core:</b> The central control unit. Only one is needed.</li><li><b>Graphite Moderators:</b> These form the bulk of the reactor assembly and are required to sustain a reaction.</li><li><b>Fuel Channels:</b> These house the fuel rods and must be connected to a coolant pipe loop.</li><li><b>Control Rod Assemblies:</b> These house the control rods used to manage the reaction.</li></ul>
All components must be wrenched down and form a single, contiguous structure around the core.<br><br>
<b><u>3. Operation</u></b><br>
1. Load fuel rods into the fuel channels.<br>
2. Load control rods into the control rod assemblies.<br>
3. Ensure a closed pipe loop with a coolant (water) is running through all fuel channels.<br>
4. Link a Reactor Control Console to the core using a multitool.<br>
5. From the console, activate the reactor.<br>
6. Carefully withdraw the control rods to increase reactivity and heat. Do not exceed the critical temperature.<br>
7. The heat will turn the coolant into steam. This steam must be fed into Steam Turbines to generate power.<br><br>
<b><u>4. SAFETY WARNINGS</u></b><br>
<ul><li><b>OVERHEATING:</b> Exceeding the core's thermal limits will cause structural damage, leading to a meltdown and catastrophic explosion.</li><li><b>POSITIVE VOID COEFFICIENT:</b> This reactor is inherently unstable. A loss of coolant will cause a rapid, uncontrolled spike in reactivity. NEVER run the reactor without active coolant flow.</li><li><b>SCRAM (АЗ-5):</b> The emergency shutdown button is a last resort. Due to a design feature, initiating a SCRAM will cause a brief but massive reactivity spike before the rods fully insert. Under high-power conditions, this can trigger a meltdown.</li><li><b>RADIATION:</b> The active core and spent fuel are highly radioactive. Always wear appropriate radiation protection.</li></ul>
<center><i>Nanotrasen is not liable for any localized reality failure resulting from improper reactor operation.</i></center>
"}
